#!spectest
# mode: run
# adjudicator: production-v1
# since: spec-v0.1
# features: []
# doc: |
#   THE MISSING-VALUE cell: `=` is OPTIONAL in the grammar, so `directive
#   scheduler` PARSES — and then fails the SET check, because `scheduler` is the
#   one directive whose value is mandatory. Rust gg renders the absent value as
#   `scheduler=(missing value)`; the self-host mirrors that spelling.
#
#   ⚠ This cell is also where `directive scheduler single` (the space-separated
#   spelling the self-host used to accept, and to act on) now lands after the
#   parser stops consuming a bare value: it becomes a valueless `scheduler`.
#
#   `directive` names a CLOSED set of four compile-time switches. The set is
#   closed by DESIGN rather than by omission: D-A36(e) ratified that lints get
#   their own `lint` keyword and never ride `directive`, so an unrecognised
#   name is a user error, not a forward-compatible extension point. The
#   normative grammar (`docs/language-reference.md`, "Directives") is
#
#       directive = "directive" IDENTIFIER { "-" IDENTIFIER } [ "=" IDENTIFIER ]
#
#   and the accompanying table fixes the four names and the four `scheduler`
#   modes. Four independent witnesses agree on that set: the book appendix
#   (`docs/book/appendix-directives.md`), the reference table + EBNF, Rust gg's
#   validator arms (`src/semantic/mod.rs`), and the consumers
#   (`src/ir/lowering/mod.rs`, `src/loader.rs`).
#
#   Until this fixture landed the self-host performed NO directive validation
#   at all — it COMPILED, LINKED and RAN a program whose directive Rust gg
#   refuses, and `self_host_lowerer/lower.gg` then discarded the value, so
#   `directive scheduler=bogus` silently ran an async program under the
#   default POOL scheduler where `=single` gives a different interleaving.
#   That is Core #10 (lower-or-reject) in its exact forbidden shape, on the
#   lane the succession plan makes the reference — which is why the verdict is
#   pinned on all three production lanes and not on the one that already had
#   it right.
#
#   adjudicator: production-v1 — `Item::Directive` has no arm anywhere in
#   ggdef's `elaborate`, so the ggdef lane records GGDEF-SKIP
#   (`item kind other is outside the phase-0 subset`, measured). Per the
#   owner's 2026-08-31 ruling ggdef ABSTAINS on directives rather than being
#   taught them; the subset gap is filed.
# expect:
#   exit: 1
#   stdout: ""
#   reject: E_UnknownDirective
#!end

directive scheduler

void main():
    print("REACHED — the directive was NOT validated on this lane")
